      <h2 id="what">What Moutai actually is</h2>
      <p>Moutai — 茅台, officially 贵州茅台酒 — is a sauce-aroma baijiu produced by Kweichow Moutai Co., Ltd. in Maotai Town, Renhuai, in northern Guizhou province. The distillery's modern corporate form dates to 1951, when the newly established state consolidated several historic workshops in the town, though the local brewing tradition is much older.</p>
      <p>Three things give the product its identity, and all three are geographic:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The water and climate.</strong> Maotai Town sits in a river valley on the Chishui (Red Water) River. The hot, humid microclimate is credited with shaping the microbial environment in which fermentation takes place.</li>
        <li><strong>The pits and the environment.</strong> Sauce-aroma fermentation relies on a microbial population that lives in the workshop environment itself — walls, tools, floors, air. This is why the appellation is legally protected: the same recipe made elsewhere is a different product.</li>
        <li><strong>The local sorghum.</strong> The red-glazed <em>hongyingzi</em> sorghum grown in the region is small-grained and tough-skinned, suited to the repeated steaming the process demands.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This is why Moutai carries a geographical-indication standard — GB/T 18356 — rather than the generic sauce-aroma standard GB/T 26760 that other producers use. The standard is not a quality claim above all others; it is a statement that the product comes from a defined place and is made a defined way.</p>

      <h2 id="process">The 12987 process</h2>
      <p>Sauce aroma is the most laborious production cycle in baijiu, and Moutai's version is the reference implementation. The industry shorthand is <strong>12987</strong>, and each number is a step count:</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Number</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1</td><td>One production year per batch</td><td>Grain goes in once a year and the cycle runs twelve months</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2</td><td>Two grain additions</td><td>Fresh sorghum is added in two stages, not all at once</td></tr>
            <tr><td>9</td><td>Nine steamings</td><td>Repeated steaming extracts starch gradually rather than in one pass</td></tr>
            <tr><td>8</td><td>Eight fermentation rounds</td><td>Each round runs about a month in stone-lined pits</td></tr>
            <tr><td>7</td><td>Seven distillate collections</td><td>Each collection has a different character and is kept separate</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>After that, the seven distillate streams are classified and stored in ceramic jars. Base spirit ages a minimum of three years; premium bottlings rest longer. Then blending begins — and this is where the craft concentrates, because the blender is combining dozens of components across rounds and years into a consistent house profile. The final blend rests again before bottling.</p>
      <p>The consequence is a production cycle measured in years, not weeks. That structural cost sits underneath the price, independent of brand premium, and it is why sauce aroma is more expensive across the entire category — Xijiu and Langjiu have the same problem.</p>

      <h2 id="taste">What it tastes like</h2>
      <p>Moutai is not an easy drink, and it was never designed to be. The profile:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Nose:</strong> fermented soybean, roasted grain, dark mushroom, dried Chinese date, a hint of caramel, sometimes sandalwood. Assertive and it fills the glass immediately.</li>
        <li><strong>Palate:</strong> full-bodied and warming. Savoury entry with fermented bean paste, roasted grain, dried plum, faint cocoa, and a distinct umami layer. At 53% the alcohol is present but integrated — it provides structure rather than burn.</li>
        <li><strong>Finish:</strong> very long, evolving from savoury to a returning sweetness with grain and herb notes. Sixty seconds or more is normal.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The closest Western comparisons are not vodka or even gin. They are peated Islay whisky, aged funky rum, or a mature sherry — spirits where a savoury, fermented, slightly medicinal complexity is the point rather than a flaw. If those are your references, Moutai will make sense quickly. If your references are clean spirits, it may take several sessions.</p>
      <blockquote><p>Moutai rewards attention and punishes speed. Drunk in small sips with rich food, it is remarkable. Drunk in shots at a banquet, it is just expensive heat.</p></blockquote>

      <h2 id="price">Why the price behaves like that</h2>
      <p>Moutai's official retail guide price for Flying Fairy 500 ml is ¥1,499. In practice, bottles have traded well above that for years, and outside China the same bottle commonly sells for $250–600 depending on market and vintage.</p>
      <p>Four forces produce that gap:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Supply is physically capped.</strong> Production is limited by the appellation — the town, the pits, the climate, and the multi-year cycle. You cannot simply build another Moutai factory somewhere else and get the same product.</li>
        <li><strong>Demand is social, not just gustatory.</strong> Moutai is the default prestige gift in Chinese business and official contexts. A significant share of bottles are bought to be given, not drunk, which sustains demand independent of how much anyone wants to drink it.</li>
        <li><strong>It functions as a store of value.</strong> Because bottles keep and prices have risen over long periods, some buyers hold stock rather than consume it. That behaviour amplifies scarcity.</li>
        <li><strong>Distribution is controlled.</strong> Allocation through official channels is limited relative to demand, so a large secondary market exists — and secondary markets are where counterfeit risk concentrates.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The practical implication for a buyer: <strong>you are not only paying for liquid.</strong> Part of the price is a social and financial premium that has nothing to do with what is in the glass. That is fine if you want the social object. It is poor value if you only want to taste sauce aroma.</p>

      <h2 id="lines">The product lines</h2>
      <p>This is where most overseas buyers get confused. "Moutai" covers a wide range, and the gap between the flagship and the entry products is enormous in both liquid and price.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Chinese</th><th>Approx. price</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Flying Fairy</td><td>飞天茅台</td><td>¥1,499 guide; $250–600 abroad</td><td>The flagship. 53% vol, 500 ml, white bottle, red ribbon. This is what people mean by "Moutai"</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Five Star</td><td>五星茅台</td><td>Similar to Flying Fairy</td><td>Same liquid, different label tradition; historically the domestic-facing mark</td></tr>
