      </div>
      <p>The honest recommendation: if you have never had sauce aroma, buy Xijiu. It will tell you whether you like the style for a fifth of the price, with almost no fraud risk. If you already know you love it and want the reference point, Moutai is worth it — from an authorised channel, with a receipt.</p>

      <h2 id="buy">Should you buy it?</h2>
      <h3>Buy it if</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You are giving a gift in a Chinese business or official context where the bottle's identity is the message.</li>
        <li>You already know you love sauce aroma and want the benchmark for comparison.</li>
        <li>You are buying for a collector who understands the market and the authentication requirements.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Do not buy it if</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You are new to baijiu and curious what the fuss is about — buy Xijiu instead.</li>
        <li>You want a bottle for a dinner party where the liquid, not the label, is the point.</li>
        <li>You are buying from a marketplace listing, a private seller, or anyone offering a discount.</li>
        <li>You are treating it as an investment without understanding storage, authentication, and liquidity risk.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you do buy, read <a href="/how-to-spot-fake-maotai/">how to spot fake Moutai</a> first. It covers the four distinct fraud types — counterfeits, refills, lookalike lines, and invented vintages — and the checks that actually work against each.</p>

      <section class="faq" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <h3>Is Moutai the same as baijiu?</h3>
        <p>No. Moutai is one brand within the sauce-aroma category, which is one of twelve aroma types within baijiu. It is the most famous producer, but treating it as the definition of the category is like treating Champagne as the definition of wine.</p>
        <h3>Why is Moutai so expensive?</h3>
        <p>Four reasons: production is physically capped by the appellation and a multi-year cycle; demand is driven by gifting and status as much as by taste; bottles function as a store of value so some buyers hold rather than drink them; and distribution through official channels is limited. Only the first of those is a cost of production.</p>
        <h3>Is Flying Fairy different from Five Star?</h3>
        <p>They are the same liquid under different labels with different historical distribution roles — Flying Fairy was long the export mark, Five Star the domestic one. Collectors treat them as distinct; drinkers generally do not need to.</p>
        <h3>Does Moutai improve with age in the bottle?</h3>
        <p>To a degree. Esterification continues slowly and the spirit softens, which is why older bottles command premiums. But the improvement is far smaller than the price premium implies, and storage conditions matter enormously. A badly stored old bottle can be worse than a fresh one.</p>
        <h3>What is the cheapest way to taste real Moutai?</h3>
        <p>Buy a series product such as Prince or Welcome — they are genuine Moutai-family sauce aroma at a fraction of the price, and they will tell you whether the house style suits you. They are not the flagship, but they are honest.</p>
        <h3>Is Moutai a good investment?</h3>
        <p>It has been historically, and it is also a speculative asset with real risks: counterfeit and refill fraud, storage damage, illiquidity, and price volatility driven by policy and fashion rather than by consumption. Treat any investment thesis as requiring specialist knowledge, not as a default assumption.</p>
